==English==
{{was wotd|2008|July|25}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sem-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|LL.|assimilātus}}, variant of {{der|en|la|assimulātus||made similar, imitated}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|assimulō}}, from {{m|la|ad}} + {{m|la|simulō||imitate, copy}}, from {{m|la|similis||like, similar}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sem-||together, one}}. {{doublet|en|assemble}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/əˈsɪm.ɪ.leɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-assimilate.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-assimilate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[incorporate]] [[nutrient]]s into the [[body]], especially after [[digestion]].
#: {{ux|en|Food is '''assimilated''' and converted into organic tissue.}}
#* {{RQ:Newton Opticks
|edition=4th|passage=Hence also it may be, that the Parts of Animals and Vegetables preserve their several Forms, and '''assimilate''' their Nourishment; [...]|book=III|page=362}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=An Inquiry Into the Opinions, Ancient and Modern, Concerning Life and Organization|passage=In the living state, the body is observed to receive [[aliment]]; to '''assimilate''' a part; to evacuate what is redundant or useless; [...]|year=1822|author=John Barclay|page=1|authorlink=John Barclay|pageurl=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/An_Inquiry_Into_the_Opinions_Ancient_and/YYEwE7FYP14C?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA1|section=I|chapter=I|publisher=Bell & Bradfute; Waugh & Innes; G. & W. B. Whittaker|location=Edinburgh; London|url=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/An_Inquiry_Into_the_Opinions_Ancient_and/YYEwE7FYP14C?hl=en&gbpv=0}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To incorporate or [[absorb]] ([[knowledge]]) into the [[mind]].
#: {{ux|en|The teacher paused in her lecture to allow the students to '''assimilate''' what she had said.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1850|author={{w|Charles Merivale}}|title=History of the Romans Under the Empire
|passage=His mind had no power to '''assimilate''' the lessons.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To absorb (a person or people) into a [[community]] or [[culture]].
#: {{ux|en|The aliens in the science-fiction film wanted to '''assimilate''' human beings into their own race.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare|used with "to" or "with"}} To [[liken]], [[compare]] to something [[similar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|text=The  use  of  an  animal  to  kill,  wound  or  threaten  is  '''assimilated'''  to  the  use  of  a  weapon.|url=https://www.legislationline.org/documents/action/popup/id/8888|publisher=Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights|translator=Spencer, J. R.|title=Penal Code [of France]|date=2005-10-12|accessdate=2020-01-19|page=25}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring to a likeness or to conformity; to cause a resemblance between.
#* '''March 13, 1866''', {{w|John Bright}}, ''The reform bill on the motion for leave to bring in the bill''
#*: to '''assimilate''' our law in respect to the law of Scotland
#* {{RQ:Cowper Task
|passage=Fast falls a fleecy shower; the downy flakes / '''Assimilate''' all objects.}}
#* {{RQ:Hale Contemplations|part=2|chapter=Meditations upon the Lord’s Prayer|page=478|passage=[I]t [the seed of life] doth, by degrees, '''aſſimilate''' the whole inward Man to this living Principle, and conforms the Life unto it.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become similar.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be incorporated or absorbed into something.

